\[STANDFIRST — one short paragraph. The question the post answers, before any method.\]

# I. What the number is

- The nonfarm payroll data measure 80% of paid workers who contribute to GDP in the U.S.[^1]
- The Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) collects payroll data from private and government entities on a monthly basis and usually publishes the data on the first Friday of each month.
- The measure excludes: farm employees, active-duty military members, unpaid volunteers for non-profit organizations, sole proprietors, and private household employees.[^2]
- The reference period for the CES survey is the pay period which includes the 12th day of the month.[^3]

[^1]: <https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/payems>

[^2]: <https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/payems>

[^3]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

## Why are certain groups excluded?

- CES draws its sample based on administrative records from the Office of Unemployment Insurance (UI). This means workers who are not covered under the government's unemployment insurance program wouldn't be counted. In agriculture, workers tend to have exemptions to requirements for unemployment coverage. In addition, businesses are frequently family-owned (i.e. sole proprietors), which make them out of scope for CES.[^4] Lastly, the sample size for farming would be too small to make statistically sound estimates (0.9%–1.2% of total employment).[^5]
- This doesn't mean that farming employment is completely unaccounted for. The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) conducts surveys twice every year to collect data on agricultural workers.
- The nonfarm payroll report primarily covers employment that is associated with the business cycle. Military employment changes primarily based on the need for national defense, not the performance of the economy. \[FINISH — self-employed individuals: why they're out of scope.\]

[^4]: BLS, *Why doesn't CES collect agricultural employment?* <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

[^5]: USDA ERS. <https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/chart-gallery/58282>

# II. How the number is made

## The sample and the census

> "Each month the CES program surveys about 119,000 businesses and government agencies, representing approximately 622,000 individual worksites... The active sample includes approximately 26 percent of all nonfarm payroll jobs."[^6]

[^6]: BLS, *Employment Situation Technical Note*, August 2026. <https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.tn.htm>

> "About 97 percent of total nonfarm employment within the scope of the establishment survey is covered by UI and is available to the CES program via QCEW records."[^7]

[^7]: BLS, *CES National Benchmark Article*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbmart.htm>

> "In 2025, BLS derived totals of 12.1 million establishments, 155.7 million employed, and $12.3 trillion in wages, from reports submitted to state workforce agencies by every employer covered by UI or by UCFE."[^17]

[^17]: BLS, *Employment and Wages, Annual Averages 2025*. <https://www.bls.gov/cew/publications/employment-and-wages-annual-averages/current/>

QCEW is published "within 6 months after the end of each referenced quarter" and covers "more than 95 percent of U.S. jobs."[^18]

[^18]: BLS, *QCEW Overview*. <https://www.bls.gov/cew/overview.htm>

\[NOTE TO SELF — 95% and 97% are different denominators, not a contradiction. 95% is of *all US jobs* (QCEW's own framing); 97% is of *nonfarm employment within the scope of the establishment survey* (the CES framing). Don't let them collide in one sentence.\]

\[WRITE — the core trade-off: CES is fast and partial, QCEW is complete and slow. Everything in Parts III and IV follows from this one design choice.\]

```{r}
#| label: tbl-ces-qcew
#| tbl-cap: "The two employment measures."
#| echo: false

library(knitr)
data.frame(
  x = c("Source", "Units covered", "Employment covered", "Frequency", "Lag"),
  ces = c("Sample survey, UI-derived frame", "~119,000 firms / ~622,000 worksites",
          "~26% of nonfarm payroll jobs", "Monthly", "~3 weeks"),
  qcew = c("Near-census of UI tax records", "~12.1 million establishments (2025)",
           "~97% of in-scope nonfarm employment", "Quarterly", "within 6 months")
) |> kable(col.names = c("", "CES", "QCEW"))
```

## The birth-death model

\[WRITE. Two components, not one:\]

1.  *Imputation* — firms that stop reporting are carried forward at the trend of continuing firms, which implicitly nets most births against deaths.
2.  *An ARIMA model on the residual only*, fitted to QCEW microdata over the trailing five years.[^8]

[^8]: BLS, *CES Net Birth-Death Model*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbd.htm>

\[The honest criticism is not "BLS invents jobs." It is that step 2 assumes the historical birth–death relationship holds, which fails exactly at turning points. Quarterly updates since January 2011 cut the forecast horizon from up to 21 months to at most 12.[^9] Effective with January 2026 estimates, BLS began feeding current sample information into the ARIMA forecasts.[^10]\]

[^9]: BLS, *Introduction of Quarterly Birth-Death Model Updates*. <https://www.bls.gov/ces/methods/ces-quarterly-birthdeath.htm>

[^10]: BLS, *Upcoming Changes to the Establishment Survey Birth-Death Model*. <https://www.bls.gov/sae/notices/2025/upcoming-changes-to-the-establishment-survey-birth-death-model.htm>

## Seasonal adjustment

Seasonal adjustments make it easier to determine whether an employment change is due to normal changes throughout the year or an indicator of anomalies.

\[REWRITE THE EXAMPLE — the old draft used farming, but nonfarm payrolls exclude agriculture. Use retail hiring in November–December, construction collapsing in winter, or education, where teachers roll off payrolls every summer.\]

\[WRITE — the mechanism:\]

- CES uses **X-13ARIMA-SEATS** with a **concurrent** methodology; models are re-specified annually.[^11]
- X-11 is the engine: an iterative sequence of moving averages, not a regression.
- X-13 wraps X-11 in a regARIMA preprocessing step handling outliers, trading-day and moving-holiday effects (CES adjusts for Easter and Labor Day), and *forecast extension* — which is why the most recent months' factors are the least stable.
- Concurrent adjustment is a revision source in its own right: the seasonally adjusted history changes every month even when no new survey responses arrive.

[^11]: BLS, *CES Seasonal Adjustment*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesseasadj.htm>

\[CHART IDEA — plot NSA against SA for one year. January NSA falls \~2.5–3 million while SA shows roughly +100k. The adjustment does 20–30× as much work as the signal.\]

# III. How the number changes

Three structurally different operations all get called "a revision," and they have different causes, sizes and meanings.

```{r}
#| label: tbl-vocab
#| tbl-cap: "Vocabulary for CES payroll estimates and their revisions."
#| echo: false

library(knitr)
data.frame(
  Colloquial = c("initial print", "first revision", "second revision, \"final\"",
                 "the big revision", "annual benchmark"),
  BLS = c("first preliminary estimate", "second preliminary estimate", "third closing",
          "preliminary benchmark estimate", "final benchmark"),
  Driver = c("—", "late survey responses", "late survey responses",
             "QCEW, March anchor only", "QCEW + rescaling + birth-death")
) |> kable(col.names = c("What you'll hear", "What BLS calls it", "What drives the change"))
```

Two traps worth naming for the reader: the "final sample-based estimate" is not final — the benchmark is still coming; and the number in the news is usually the *preliminary* benchmark, which itself gets revised.

## The monthly cycle

CES "revises its initial monthly estimates twice, in the immediately succeeding 2 months, to incorporate additional sample receipts from respondents in the survey."[^12]

[^12]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

![](fig-release-schedule.png)

\[WRITE — July 2026 is the example: the initial print said the economy *lost* 23,000 jobs; one month of additional survey responses flipped it to a *gain* of 21,000. Same month, same method, opposite sign.\]

## The annual benchmark

The final benchmark is published with the January preliminary estimates in early February, and affects **21 months** of previously published data, anchored to **March of the previous year**.[^13]

[^13]: BLS, *CES Frequently Asked Questions*. <https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesfaq.htm>

![](fig-benchmark-window.png)

\[WRITE — the split almost nobody mentions: the 12 months up to the March anchor get the QCEW treatment; the 9 months after it are only rescaled and re-modelled. BofA states the consequence plainly: April–December 2025 payrolls "will not be revised based on the QCEW until 2027."[^14] So a payroll number is roughly eighteen months old before anyone checks it against tax records.\]

[^14]: BofA Global Research, *US Economic Viewpoint — QCEW: 2025 FAQs*, 3 September 2025.

![](fig-successive-windows.png)

\[WRITE — Figure 3 is the payoff: April–December 2025 sits in both windows. Pale in February 2026 (modelled), rust in February 2027 (measured). Same nine months, two completely different treatments, a year apart.\]

Also worth folding in: **five years of seasonally adjusted data are recalculated** with every benchmark.[^15] Months far outside the 21-month window change every February from seasonal factors alone.

[^15]: BLS, *Employment Situation Quick Guide*. <https://www.bls.gov/bls/empsitquickguide.htm>

# IV. What happened in 2024–2026

| Benchmark (to March) | Preliminary | Final              |
|----------------------|-------------|--------------------|
| March 2024           | −818,000    | −598,000           |
| March 2025           | −911,000    | −862,000           |
| March 2026           | −79,000     | *pending Feb 2027* |

\[WRITE — the arc. A −911k revision fed a year of "the data is fake" arguments. BLS changed the birth-death model effective January 2026. The next preliminary benchmark came in at −79,000 and was released on 28 August 2026 to near-silence.\]

\[THE OPEN QUESTION YOU CAN TAKE A POSITION ON — is that the model fix working, or just a calmer labour market? Note the Cleveland Fed finds 1980–2025 benchmark revisions have a mean of −4.51k and a median of −2.00k, and that structural-break tests find *no break* — so 2024–25 was extreme but not a regime change.[^16]\]

[^16]: Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, *BLS Benchmark Revisions: Is This Time Different?*, 2026. <https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/economic-commentary/2026/ec-202612-bls-benchmark-revisions-is-this-time-different>

\[CAVEATS — what this cannot answer.\]

## Questions worth further exploration

**Is the CES sample shrinking, and does it matter?** The August 2026 technical note puts the sample at 119,000 businesses and 622,000 worksites, against 121,000 and 631,000 a year earlier — a decline of roughly 1.5%. Over the same period the stated coverage moved from "about one-third" of nonfarm payroll jobs to "approximately 26 percent." A 1.5% reduction in the sample cannot produce a shift of that size, so either the denominator changed or BLS restated the basis. \[Worth running down — if the sample really is contracting, the standard error on every monthly print is widening, and nobody is writing about it.\]

